///| `src/sshcopyid` -- the rules of `ssh-copy-id`, without the transport.
///
/// Loop #103.  Upstream's `ssh-copy-id` is a POSIX shell script that installs
/// public keys into a remote account's `authorized_keys`.  This package holds
/// everything about that tool that is a **rule** rather than a transport:
///
///   * `args.mbt`  -- the option table (upstream's getopts string
///     `"io:p:F:t:fnsxh?"`) and the refusals it prints,
///   * `msg.mbt`   -- the sentences, read out of the real script's own bytes
///     (captured by `host/copyid_probe.sh`, compared by
///     `host/verify_loop103.mjs`),
///   * `install.mbt` -- the remote command, in the two shapes a server can
///     actually run,
///   * `flow.mbt`  -- the order of the steps and the exit status they leave.
///
/// ## Why the sentences live here and not in the driver
///
/// The driver in `cmd/ssh_copy_id` may translate between our error types and
/// the words OpenSSH prints.  It may not decide anything.  A sentence that
/// lives in the driver is a sentence no unit test can pin and no mutation can
/// aim at, and this tool's entire observable surface on a POSIX server is its
/// sentences: the install itself happens on the far side of a connection.
///
/// ## What is measured and what is copied
///
/// The upstream script is 368 lines of `/bin/sh` and it cannot run on a
/// Windows server at all -- measured, not guessed: it exits 0, prints
/// "Number of key(s) added: 1", and the key is never written, because the
/// Windows `sshd` runs remote commands through `cmd.exe /c "..."` and the
/// script's `exec sh -c '...'` is not a command there.
///
/// So the sentences and the POSIX command are **copied from upstream's own
/// captured bytes** (the oracle is `host/copyid_probe.sh`, which runs the
/// real script with a stand-in `ssh` and keeps every byte it produces, and
/// `host/verify_loop103.mjs`, which compares the two byte for byte),
/// and the Windows command is **measured**: `more >> file` was tried and
/// rejected because it appends two CRLFs the input did not have, and
/// `powershell -NoProfile -EncodedCommand` was measured to append the
/// input byte for byte.
///
/// ## The one deliberate difference
///
/// Upstream reports success it did not check for.  This tool authenticates
/// with the key it just installed and says so when the server does not
/// accept it.  The divergence is in `flow.mbt` and it is the reason this
/// loop exists at all.
import {
  "moonbitlang/core/encoding/base64",
  "moonbitlang/core/encoding/utf8",
}

pkgtype(kind: "library")
